event: response.output_text.done
data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_0450f9c75850a39c016a68cb2c95bc81949cc489ac4fc6f08e","logprobs":[],"output_index":1,"sequence_number":290,"text":"PASS\n\nASSIGNED-TRANSITION VERDICTS\nfalse belief named: OK\ncredit removed or reassigned: OK\nsacrifice removed: OK\nassigned reframe settled: OK\nreader does the work: OK\nscare-then-disown: NONE ASSIGNED\n\n**Overall belief-change assessment:** The chapter completes its assigned opening transition with force comparable to the real chapter. It precisely names the reader’s feared future—another regime of “plans, boundaries, resets, streaks” sustained by discipline—and contrasts that with ending the exhausting cycle of “anticipating, bargaining, and starting over.” It reassigns the limited credit due to controlled eating by acknowledging that structure can produce real relief while asking whether its continuing maintenance is freedom or merely “a well-managed truce.” Sacrifice is removed at the level appropriate to this entry contract: the reader is told to eat normally, begin no new restriction, retain autonomy, and investigate whether BAD SUGAR possesses any irreplaceable benefit. Questions such as “Does any of that sound like freedom?” and “Does BAD SUGAR deliver a special, irreplaceable pleasure?” make the reader participate rather than merely accept a lecture. The destination is also settled clearly as no longer wanting or negotiating over BAD SUGAR, “without deprivation, without battle, and without a lifetime of rules,” while the detailed demolition of its supposed benefits is properly reserved for later chapters."}

